RISCV IPC-WAIT-PENDING-MP-WEAK
(*
 * Narrowing proof for the WAIT_PENDING handshake in kernel/ipc/fastmsg.c.  The
 * real code uses a full `cpu_smp_mb()` (fence rw,rw) on both the waiter publish
 * side and the waker observe side.  This variant replaces them with the minimal
 * message-passing fences: `fence w,w` on the waiter (StoreStore: data stores
 * before the flag clear) and `fence r,r` on the waker (LoadLoad: flag load
 * before the state loads).  It proves the lost-wakeup outcome stays `Never`
 * under RVWMO, i.e. the ISOLATED WAIT_PENDING bridge-bit handshake could use
 * w,w / r,r instead of the full rw,rw.
 *
 * Scope: this licenses narrowing the publish-side fence to `fence w,w`.  It does
 * NOT license narrowing the observe-side fences in the code, because several of
 * those sites also order a prior MSG_DELIVERY claim store (`START_SMP_XFER`)
 * that a bare `fence r,r` would not; that handoff is outside this MP model.  See
 * the barrier-audit table in docs/smp-contract.md.
 *
 * Even the proven publish narrowing is intentionally NOT applied: `cpu_smp_mb()`
 * is one shared macro used across the tree, and `measure or revert` (docs
 * Standing Gate) requires a measured throughput/latency win before trading
 * uniformity for a bespoke narrower fence; none has been shown.
 *)
{
0:x1=data; 0:x2=flag;
1:x1=flag; 1:x2=data;
flag=1; data=0;
}

P0            | P1            ;
li x5,1       | lw x7,0(x1)   ;
sw x5,0(x1)   | fence r,r     ;
fence w,w     | lw x8,0(x2)   ;
sw x0,0(x2)   |               ;

exists (1:x7=0 /\ 1:x8=0)
